          "tipo": "gramatica",
          "min": 12,
          "titulo": "Gender and the four articles",
          "explicacao": "STEP 1: in Portuguese every noun is masculine or feminine, even objects. The article tells you which: «o livro» (masculine), «a casa» (feminine). The gender has nothing to do with the meaning: «a mesa» (table) is feminine, «o sol» (sun) is masculine. Always learn a new noun together with its article. STEP 2: the definite article («the») has four forms: «o» (masculine singular), «a» (feminine singular), «os» (masculine plural), «as» (feminine plural). The indefinite article («a, an, some») also has four forms: «um, uma, uns, umas». «Uns» and «umas» mean «some», «a few». STEP 3: a simple rule for gender: nouns in «-o» are usually masculine («o livro, o carro») and nouns in «-a» are usually feminine («a casa, a mesa»). Nouns in «-ção», «-são», «-dade» and «-gem» are feminine («a lição, a cidade, a viagem»). Nouns in «-or» are usually masculine («o amor, o professor», but «a flor»). STEP 4: the exceptions you must memorize: «o dia, o mapa, o problema, o sistema, o programa, o tema, o planeta, o idioma» are masculine even though they end in «-a», and «a mão» (hand) is feminine even though it ends in «-o». STEP 5: Portuguese uses «the» more than English. You use it with names of countries («o Brasil, a Alemanha»), with a general subject («O café é bom» = coffee is good) and, in speech, often with first names («a Ana, o Pedro»). It is not wrong to say just «Ana». You still do not use «um/uma» before a bare profession («Sou professor»). STEP 6: adjectives agree with the noun: «um livro novo», «uma casa nova», «o carro caro», «a mala cara». The adjective usually comes after the noun.",
          "comparacao": "English has one «the» and no gender, and «a/an» is only a matter of sound. In Portuguese the article is the key to the gender of the noun and it changes with the number. Tip: when you learn «mesa», learn «a mesa». There is no way to guess every gender, but the endings above work most of the time.",
